steps.body:
SIMD (single instruction, multiple data) instructions work on several values at once. Every x86-64 CPU has SSE2: 16 registers of 128 bits (`xmm`), each holding 2 doubles or 4 ints. Most x86 servers built since 2013 also have AVX2, whose 256-bit `ymm` registers hold 4 doubles. AVX-512 doubles that again on some servers, but the sandbox's CPU stops at AVX2. Apple Silicon has NEON instead, 128 bits like SSE2.

Computing mids over a day of quotes stored as a struct of arrays, the layout from the lesson on memory layout, is the textbook case:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}

int main() {
    double bid[3] = {100.0, 100.5, 101.0};
    double ask[3] = {100.5, 101.0, 101.25};
    double mid[3];
    mids(bid, ask, mid, 3);
    std::cout << mid[0] << ' ' << mid[2] << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
void mids(const double* bid, const double* ask, double* mid, std::size_t n) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        mid[i] = (bid[i] + ask[i]) * 0.5;
    }
}
```

g++ 10 doesn't vectorize at `-O2`: the loop handles one double per `addsd` and `mulsd`. `-O3` turns on the vectorizer, and the same loop handles two per `addpd` and `mulpd`, or four per `vaddpd` with `-mavx2`. g++ 12 began vectorizing cheap loops at `-O2`, and Apple clang already does, so on a Mac `-O2` already gives NEON `fadd.2d` code, and fuses any multiply-add into `fmla.2d`, which can change the last digits. In the sandbox, make the first line of the file exactly `// -O3`, which builds it the way `g++ -O3` would.

```text
; g++ -O2: one double per instruction
.L3:
    movsd   xmm0, QWORD PTR [rdi+rax*8]
    addsd   xmm0, QWORD PTR [rsi+rax*8]
    mulsd   xmm0, xmm1
    movsd   QWORD PTR [rdx+rax*8], xmm0
    add     rax, 1

; g++ -O3: two doubles per instruction
.L4:
    movupd  xmm0, XMMWORD PTR [rdi+rax]
    movupd  xmm2, XMMWORD PTR [rsi+rax]
    addpd   xmm0, xmm2
    mulpd   xmm0, xmm1
    movups  XMMWORD PTR [rdx+rax], xmm0
    add     rax, 16
```

Each lane does the same IEEE add and multiply as the scalar loop, so the results match bit for bit. Sums are different. A `double` total must add in order, and 4 partial sums round differently, so g++ keeps `addsd` even at `-O3` unless `-ffast-math` allows reordering. An integer sum has no rounding, so it vectorizes, with `paddq`.

example.code:
// -O3
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void mids(const double* bid, const double* ask, double* mid, std::size_t n) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        mid[i] = (bid[i] + ask[i]) * 0.5;  // vectorized at -O3: addpd, mulpd
    }
}

__attribute__((optimize("no-tree-vectorize")))
void mids_scalar(const double* bid, const double* ask, double* mid, std::size_t n) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        mid[i] = (bid[i] + ask[i]) * 0.5;  // one double at a time: addsd, mulsd
    }
}

std::int64_t total_size(const std::int64_t* size, std::size_t n) {
    std::int64_t total = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        total += size[i];  // an integer sum vectorizes too: paddq
    }
    return total;
}

int main() {
    const std::size_t n = 1003;  // odd, so the leftover tail runs too
    std::vector<double> bid(n), ask(n), fast(n), slow(n);
    std::vector<std::int64_t> size(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        bid[i] = (10000 + i % 97) / 100.0;
        ask[i] = (10001 + i % 97 + i % 3) / 100.0;
        size[i] = 100 + i * 37 % 900;
    }
    mids(bid.data(), ask.data(), fast.data(), n);
    mids_scalar(bid.data(), ask.data(), slow.data(), n);

    const bool same = std::memcmp(fast.data(), slow.data(), n * sizeof(double)) == 0;
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (double m : fast) {
        sum += m;
    }
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(6);
    std::cout << "vector mids == scalar mids, bit for bit: " << (same ? "yes" : "no") << '\n';
    std::cout << "first mid " << fast[0] << ", last mid " << fast[n - 1] << '\n';
    std::cout << "sum of mids " << sum << ", total size " << total_size(size.data(), n) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

