When the vectorizer won't, write the vector instructions yourself with **intrinsics** from `<immintrin.h>`: functions that each compile to one instruction. `__m256d` holds 4 doubles in a `ymm` register:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

__attribute__((target("avx2")))
void mids_avx2(const double* bid, const double* ask, double* mid, std::size_t n) {
    {{code}}
}

int main() {
    double bid[6] = {100.0, 100.5, 101.0, 101.5, 102.0, 102.5};
    double ask[6] = {100.5, 101.0, 101.5, 102.0, 102.5, 103.5};
    double mid[6];
    if (__builtin_cpu_supports("avx2")) {
        mids_avx2(bid, ask, mid, 6);
    } else {
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
            mid[i] = (bid[i] + ask[i]) * 0.5;
        }
    }
    std::cout << mid[0] << ' ' << mid[5] << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
const __m256d half = _mm256_set1_pd(0.5);    // 0.5 in all 4 lanes
std::size_t i = 0;
for (; i + 4 <= n; i += 4) {                 // loadu and storeu take any alignment
    const __m256d sum = _mm256_add_pd(_mm256_loadu_pd(bid + i), _mm256_loadu_pd(ask + i));
    _mm256_storeu_pd(mid + i, _mm256_mul_pd(sum, half));
}
for (; i < n; ++i) {
    mid[i] = (bid[i] + ask[i]) * 0.5;        // the leftover tail
}
```

`_mm256_sub_pd` and `_mm256_div_pd` work the same way, and the SSE2 versions (`__m128d`, `_mm_add_pd`, 2 doubles) work in any x86-64 build. AVX2 needs the compiler's permission: `-mavx2` for a whole file, or `#pragma GCC target("avx2")` for the functions between `#pragma GCC push_options` and `#pragma GCC pop_options`, or `__attribute__((target("avx2")))` on one function. AVX2 code on a CPU without AVX2 dies with an illegal instruction, so ask at run time with `__builtin_cpu_supports("avx2")` and keep a fallback. MSVC lets you call any intrinsic without a flag, and `/arch:AVX2` lets its own generated code use AVX2.

`target("avx2")` leaves FMA off. With `-mfma` or `-march=native`, g++ may fuse `a * b + c` into one instruction that rounds once, so a result can move in the last bit. Apple clang on ARM fuses by default. `-ffp-contract=off` keeps every compiler to separate operations.

None of the x86 intrinsics compile on a Mac, whose ARM chip has NEON instead (`<arm_neon.h>`, `float64x2_t`, `vaddq_f64`). For one source that builds everywhere, GCC and clang's vector extensions compile to SSE2, AVX2, or NEON, whichever the target has. A `typedef`, the older spelling of a `using` alias, names the type, and `__attribute__((vector_size(32)))` makes it 32 bytes of doubles that support `+`, `*`, and `[i]`:

```cpp
typedef double v4d __attribute__((vector_size(32)));   // 4 doubles
v4d b = {100.0, 100.5, 101.0, 101.5};
v4d a = {100.5, 101.0, 101.5, 102.5};
v4d m = (b + a) * 0.5;
std::cout << m[0] << ' ' << m[3] << '\n';   // 100.25 102
```

